message.content:
This is a purely mechanical epicyclic gear reducer used as an aircraft part. Heading 8483 covers gearing and gear boxes, and the specific subheading for aircraft gearing would be 8483.40 ("gears and gearing... speed changers"). I recall a CBP ruling on a similar Boeing 747 flap actuator that classified it in 8483.40.5010. That is the "Gears and gearing, other than toothed wheels...; ball or roller screws; gear boxes and other speed changers: Other: Other" line. Subheading 8483.40.50 is "Torque converters and other speed changers", and its statistical suffix 10 is "Aircraft"... I am not certain of the suffix wording, so I am relying on memory of the ruling.

Chapter 88 (8803) parts of aircraft is a possible alternative, but Section XVI Note 1(m) excludes articles of Chapter 88, and 8483 is a general-purpose-machinery heading. Note 2 to Section XVI sends parts to the specific heading when they are goods of 8483, so 8483 prevails. 8803 covers only parts of aircraft in 8801 and 8802, and Chapter 88 Note 1 does not exclude 8483. The ruling is therefore likely correct. Gear boxes of heading 8483 are not excluded from Section XVII, since Note 2 of Section XVII excludes "machines of heading 8483" from parts of vehicles, aircraft, etc.

{"hts_code": "8483.40.5010", "reason": "A purely mechanical epicyclic/planetary gear reducer (speed changer) is classified in heading 8483, which Section XVII Note 2 keeps out of the aircraft-parts heading 8803, and 8483.40.50 covers other speed changers, with the 10 statistical suffix for aircraft use."}
